---
title: Pumpkin Pie
description: Pumpkin pie sweetened with a can of dulce de leche, in a gingersnap crust under salty cream cheese whip.
tags: [pie, baking, fall]
course: dessert
icons: [pumpkin, pie, cookie]
difficulty: easy
author: Sohla El-Waylly
source:
  name: Hot Dish with Sohla
  url: https://sohla.substack.com/p/a-pumpkin-pie-even-i-can-love
why:
  - The filling is a custard. The milk proteins in dulce de leche get in the way of the egg proteins, which gives you a few more minutes before it overcooks.
  - Spices need direct contact with fat. Blooming them in butter gets at flavors the oven alone won't.
  - Cream cheese keeps the whipped cream stable for hours.
---

= Crust

Heat the oven to 325°F (165°C). In a #food processor{}(or a zip-top bag and rolling pin), process the @gingersnaps{170%g}(about 1 1/2 cups crumbs), @granulated sugar{25%g}(2 tbsp), and @fine salt{1/2%tsp} until very finely ground. Drizzle over the @unsalted butter{56%g}(4 tbsp, melted) and pulse to combine.

> **No Food Processor** — Put the gingersnaps in a zip-top bag and smash them finely with a rolling pin. Move the crumbs to a bowl, add the sugar, salt, and butter, and mix with a stiff silicone spatula until thoroughly combined.

Press the crumb mixture into a standard 9-inch #pie pan{}, using the backs of your fingers to press it evenly along the sides and bottom. Use the flat bottom and sides of a #measuring cup{} to pack it in further.

Bake until lightly browned, set, and dry, about ~bake{20%minutes}. Transfer to a #wire rack{} to cool completely, at least ~cool{30%minutes}.

> **Making Ahead** — Tightly wrapped, the crust keeps at room temperature for 1 week or in the freezer for 1 month.

= Filling

Heat the oven to 325°F (165°C). In a #bowl{}, whisk together the @pumpkin puree{1%can}(15 oz), @dulce de leche{1%can}(14 oz), @large eggs{2}, @large egg yolk{1}, and @&fine salt{1/2%tsp}.

In a #small saucepan{}, melt the remaining @&unsalted butter{56%g} (4 tbsp) over medium heat until foamy. Add the @pumpkin pie spice{1 1/2%tbsp} and stir constantly until aromatic, about ~bloom{30%seconds}. Scrape into the pumpkin mixture and whisk to combine.

Set the cooled crust on a #sheet pan{}, then strain the filling through a #fine-mesh strainer{} into it.

Bake until the sides are set but the center is still wobbly, ~bake{42-45%minutes}. Start checking vigilantly after 40 minutes. It goes from far too wobbly to totally set in just a few minutes.

> **If It Cracks** — Bake it until fully set and the filling might crack once it cools. It'll still taste good. Cover the crack with the cream.

Cool for at least ~cool{2%hours} at room temperature before topping with the cream.

> **Storing** — The pie can chill in the fridge for up to 24 hours. After that the crust begins to lose its snap, though it's still food-safe for up to 4 days.

= Topping

Stir together the @cream cheese{6%oz}(softened), the remaining @&granulated sugar{25%g} (2 tbsp), @vanilla paste{1%tsp}(or extract), and @&fine salt{1/4%tsp} until smooth.

Stir in a few tablespoons of the @heavy whipping cream{1 1/2%cup} to loosen it up, then add the rest and whip by hand, with a hand mixer, or with a stand mixer until thick and holding soft peaks. Take care not to over-whip.

Taste and add up to 1/4 tsp more salt if needed. Every brand of cream cheese is different.

Mound or pipe the cream onto the pie. It won't weep, so pile it on and store the pie in the fridge until serving.
